body:
This results in invalid IR:

```Futhark
module M
  : {
      type arr [n]
      val mk : (n: i64) -> arr [n]
    } = {
  type arr [n] = ([n]i32, [n]i32)

  def mk n : arr [n] = let xs = replicate n 0 in (xs, xs)
}

entry main n : *M.arr [] = M.mk n
```

It is a slight modification of the test program for #2324, which focused on not *inferring* the type above, but nothing prevents us from writing it explicitly. The root problem is that the value produced by `M.mk n` might have (and *does have* in this case) "self-aliasing" (not a concept we have in the type system), which means it is invalid to treat it as a pair of two unique arrays (which would be done by desugaring of the above program).

The underlying problem is that our semantic objects for aliasing do not have a way to represent "this object is confusing and may not be consumed". We need this to support LMAD slicing anyway, and perhaps it is also the best way to handle abstract types produced by functions that do not guarantee uniqueness. Note that if `M.mk` was explicitly annotated to return a *unique* result, then matters would be fine (although in that case the concrete definition above would be ill-typed).

So the solution is to expand our aliasing lattice with a new top element that basically means "this might alias anything or everything, you cannot consume it".

Arrays of record values with named field types have projection types that are unnamed. For example here
```
    "[](v3, v3)": {
      "ctype": "struct futhark_opaque_arr1d_tup2_v3_v3 *",
      "kind": "opaque",
      "ops": {
        "free": "futhark_free_opaque_arr1d_tup2_v3_v3",
        "restore": "futhark_restore_opaque_arr1d_tup2_v3_v3",
        "store": "futhark_store_opaque_arr1d_tup2_v3_v3"
      },
      "record_array": {
        "elemtype": "(v3, v3)",
        "fields": [
          {
            "name": "0",
            "project": "futhark_project_opaque_arr1d_tup2_v3_v3_0",
            "type": "[](f32, f32, f32)"
          },
          {
            "name": "1",
            "project": "futhark_project_opaque_arr1d_tup2_v3_v3_1",
            "type": "[](f32, f32, f32)"
          }
        ],
        "index": "futhark_index_opaque_arr1d_tup2_v3_v3",
        "new": "futhark_new_opaque_arr1d_tup2_v3_v3",
        "rank": 1,
        "set": "futhark_set_opaque_arr1d_tup2_v3_v3",
        "shape": "futhark_shape_opaque_arr1d_tup2_v3_v3",
        "zip": "futhark_zip_opaque_arr1d_tup2_v3_v3"
      }
    }
```
the projection functions give values of `[](f32, f32, f32)`, instead of `[]v3` which would be more consistent with the element type. This appears to not be an issue with the record types themselves and indeed the element type `(v3, v3)` contains all necessary type information.

```Futhark
module type mod = {
  type^ fun

  val mk : i64 -> fun

  val app : fun -> i64 -> bool
}

module mod : mod = {
  type^ fun = {f: i64 -> bool}

  def mk (n: i64) : fun = {f = \x -> n == x}

  def app (fun: fun) (n: i64) : bool =
    fun.f n
}

entry prop_record_sums_succ (input: mod.fun) : bool =
  mod.app input 0
```

This should not be accepted, as `mod.fun` may be a function. However, we do not currently maintain information about liftedness in `TypeBase`, only in the type checker environment, so it is perhaps a bit annoying to fix.
